WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:17.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:25.300
K. MICHAEL HAYS: Architecture, from its very beginning, has been made of stone, and when it hasn't been

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.910
made of stone, it's been made to look like it's made of stone.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:35.480
Windows may have glass in them, but windows in a stone wall are simply openings.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.120
The glass itself is not important.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.830
The development of technologies that allow the production of large sheets of glass and

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:49.010
the materials with tensile strength, like steel, had the power to enormously change

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.180
the way buildings are made.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.660
But how would that necessarily affect architecture?

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.837
How would that change architecture's

00:00:56.837 --> 00:00:58.410
representational function?

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:04.229
How would that change architecture as the art of building?

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:09.270
The advances in modern technology and the invention of new materials

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:15.530
were not an inevitable helpful contributor to the goals of architecture culture.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:21.810
How can one apply a symbolic language, an already existing symbolic architectural language

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:27.950
that was developed over thousands of years as an expression of the heavy compressive

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.360
forces in masonry, to lightweight

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.259
and thin structures

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.359
of metal and glass?

00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:42.520
How does one achieve the requisite monumentality and profundity with such flimsy materials?

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.159
This was the primary problem for the architects of the 20th century.

